<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Venad</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1729 CE (approx.) · capital Kollam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern Kerala kingdom centred on the port of Kollam, a pepper-trading state that was reshaped by Marthanda Varma into the kingdom of Travancore.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Venad emerged in the 9th century as a chiefdom under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a>. The Tharisapalli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of 849, in which the Venad chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyanadikal-thiruvadikal">Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal</a> granted land and privileges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians">Syrian Christian</a> church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, are among the earliest records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. After the Chera Perumal state broke up in the early 12th century, Venad became independent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a> (Quilon) was a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> port, visited by Chinese and Arab merchants and later by the Portuguese, who built a fort there in the early 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-varma-kulasekhara">Ravi Varma Kulasekhara</a> (r. 1299–1314) briefly campaigned as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. The ruling family split into several branches, and real power often lay with local nobles and temple corporations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1720s and 1730s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a> crushed the nobles, absorbed neighbouring chiefdoms and transformed Venad into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travancore">Travancore</a>, the register's successor state from 1729. Its rulers remained in power until 1949.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 800 CE – 1729 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:18.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1729 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kollam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal, Ravi Varma Kulasekhara, Marthanda Varma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Venad, Kingdom of Quilon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1264&amp;focus=kingdom-of-venad" aria-label="Kingdom of Venad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6795 1846 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Venad lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7115 2102L7126 2117"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7126 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7115 2102h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7115 2102h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kollam. <a href="/atlas?year=1264&amp;focus=kingdom-of-venad">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
